<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burhanpur</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Burhanpur is a city on the Tapti river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Founded around 1400 by Nasir Khan of the Faruqi dynasty, it was the capital of the Khandesh Sultanate and, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s conquest of 1601, a major Mughal base for campaigns in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. Mumtaz Mahal died there in 1631, and the city was known for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> textiles and trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burhanpur.jpg" alt="Burhanpur" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pierre Dalbéra · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dargah-e-Hakimi_(Burhanpur),_February_2015.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
